# description : This .audit is designed against the CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 STIG Benchmark v1.0.0
#
#<ui_metadata>
#<display_name>CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 STIG v1.0.0 CAT I</display_name>
#<spec>
#  <type>CIS</type>
#  <name>Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 STIG</name>
#  <profile>CAT I</profile>
#  <version>1.0.0</version>
#  <link>https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403</link>
#</spec>
#<labels>agent,rhel_linux_10</labels>
#<benchmark_refs>800-53,800-53r5,CAT,CCI,LEVEL,Rule-ID,STIG-ID,Vuln-ID</benchmark_refs>
#<variables>
#  <variable>
#    <name>PLATFORM_VERSION</name>
#    <default>10</default>
#    <description>Linux Distribution Version</description>
#    <info>Linux Distribution Version</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#  <variable>
#    <name>PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION</name>
#    <default>SHA512</default>
#    <description>Password encryption method</description>
#    <info>Password encryption method found in /etc/login.defs</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#  <variable>
#    <name>AIDE_CONF</name>
#    <default>/etc/aide.conf</default>
#    <description>AIDE Configuration File</description>
#    <info>Full path to the aide.conf file.</info>
#    <value_type>UNIX_FILE_PATH</value_type>
#  </variable>
#  <variable>
#    <name>FIPS_POLICY</name>
#    <default>FIPS</default>
#    <description>FIPS Crypto Policy and submodule</description>
#    <info>Valid values include FIPS, FIPS:AD-SUPPORT, FIPS:NO-ENFORCE-EMS</info>
#    <value_type>STRING</value_type>
#  </variable>
#</variables>
#</ui_metadata>

<check_type:"Unix">

<if>
  <condition type:"AND">
    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 is installed"
      file        : "/etc/system-release"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*Red Hat Enterprise Linux.*release"
      expect      : "^[\\s]*Red Hat Enterprise Linux.*release @PLATFORM_VERSION@"
    </custom_item>
  </condition>

  <then>
    <report type:"PASSED">
      description : "CIS_Red_Hat_Enterprise_Linux_10_STIG_v1.0.0_CAT_I.audit from CIS Red Hat Enterprise Linux 10 STIG v1.0.0"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      show_output : YES
    </report>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.1 RHEL-10-000500"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must enable FIPS mode.

GROUP ID: V-281009
RULE ID: SV-281009r1184724

Use of weak or untested encryption algorithms undermines the purposes of using encryption to protect data. The operating system must implement cryptographic modules adhering to the higher standards approved by the federal government because this provides assurance they have been tested and validated.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000033-GPOS-00014, SRG-OS-000125-GPOS-00065, SRG-OS-000250-GPOS-00093, SRG-OS-000393-GPOS-00173, SRG-OS-000394-GPOS-00174, SRG-OS-000396-GPOS-00176, SRG-OS-000423-GPOS-00187, SRG-OS-000478-GPOS-00223"
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to implement FIPS mode.

If this check fails on an installed system, it is a permanent finding until the system is reinstalled with \"fips=1\" during installation.

Red Hat 10 does not support switching to strict FIPS mode after installation."
      reference   : "800-171|3.1.13,800-171|3.7.5,800-171|3.13.8,800-171|3.13.11,800-171r3|03.07.05,800-171r3|03.07.05b.,800-171r3|03.13.08,800-171r3|03.13.11,800-53|AC-17(2),800-53|MA-4c.,800-53|MA-4(6),800-53|SC-8,800-53|SC-13,800-53r5|AC-17(2),800-53r5|MA-4c.,800-53r5|MA-4(6),800-53r5|SC-8,800-53r5|SC-13b.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000068,CCI|CCI-000877,CCI|CCI-001453,CCI|CCI-002418,CCI|CCI-002450,CCI|CCI-002890,CCI|CCI-003123,CN-L3|7.1.2.7(g),CN-L3|7.1.3.1(d),CN-L3|8.1.2.2(a),CN-L3|8.1.2.2(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.1(c),CN-L3|8.1.4.7(a),CN-L3|8.1.4.8(a),CN-L3|8.2.4.5(c),CN-L3|8.2.4.5(d),CN-L3|8.5.2.2,CSF|PR.AC-3,CSF|PR.DS-2,CSF|PR.DS-5,CSF|PR.MA-2,CSF|PR.PT-4,CSF2.0|PR.AA-05,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,CSF2.0|PR.DS-02,CSF2.0|PR.DS-10,GDPR|32.1.a,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(iv),HIPAA|164.312(e)(1),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(ii),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.14,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ISO-27001-2022|A.6.7,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.20,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.24,ISO/IEC-27001|A.6.2.2,ISO/IEC-27001|A.10.1.1,ITSG-33|AC-17(2),ITSG-33|MA-4c.,ITSG-33|MA-4(6),ITSG-33|SC-8,ITSG-33|SC-8a.,ITSG-33|SC-13,ITSG-33|SC-13a.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|M5.2.6,NESA|T2.3.4,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,NESA|T4.5.1,NESA|T4.5.2,NESA|T5.4.2,NESA|T5.4.4,NESA|T7.3.3,NESA|T7.4.1,NIAv2|AM37,NIAv2|CY3,NIAv2|CY4,NIAv2|CY5b,NIAv2|CY5c,NIAv2|CY5d,NIAv2|CY7,NIAv2|IE8,NIAv2|IE9,NIAv2|IE12,NIAv2|NS5e,NIAv2|NS29,NIAv2|SS24,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.3,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|4.1,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.7,PCI-DSSv4.0|4.2.1,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,Rule-ID|SV-281009r1184724_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-000500,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.6,TBA-FIISB|45.2.3,Vuln-ID|V-281009"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/proc/sys/crypto/fips_enabled"
      regex       : "^1$"
      expect      : "^1$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.11 RHEL-10-001030"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must check the GNU Privacy Guard (GPG) signature of software packages originating from external software repositories before installation.

GROUP ID: V-280932
RULE ID: SV-280932r1197215

Changes to any software components can have significant effects on the overall security of the operating system. This requirement ensures the software has not been tampered with and has been provided by a trusted vendor.

All software packages must be signed with a cryptographic key recognized and approved by the organization.

Verifying the authenticity of software prior to installation validates the integrity of the software package received from a vendor."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 dnf to always check the GPG signature of software packages originating from external software repositories before installation.

Add or update the following line in the [main] section of the \"/etc/dnf/dnf.conf\" file:

gpgcheck=1"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.5,800-171r3|03.04.05,800-53|CM-5(3),800-53r5|CM-14,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-003992,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.19,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ITSG-33|CM-5(3),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.3,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.1,NESA|T7.5.3,NESA|T7.6.3,QCSC-v1|7.2,Rule-ID|SV-280932r1197215_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-001030,Vuln-ID|V-280932"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/dnf/dnf.conf"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*gpgcheck[\\s]*="
      expect      : "^[\\s]*gpgcheck[\\s]*=[\\s]*1[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.12 RHEL-10-001040"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must check the GNU Privacy Guard (GPG) signature of locally installed software packages before installation.

GROUP ID: V-280933RULE ID: SV-280933r1197217

Changes to any software components can have significant effects on the overall security of the operating system. This requirement ensures the software has not been tampered with and that it has been provided by a trusted vendor.

All software packages must be signed with a cryptographic key recognized and approved by the organization.

Verifying the authenticity of software prior to installation validates the integrity of the software package received from a vendor."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 dnf to always check the GPG signature of local software packages before installation.

Add or update the following line in the [main] section of the \"/etc/dnf/dnf.conf\" file:

localpkg_gpgcheck=1"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.5,800-171r3|03.04.05,800-53|CM-5(3),800-53r5|CM-14,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-003992,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.19,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ITSG-33|CM-5(3),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.3,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.1,NESA|T7.5.3,NESA|T7.6.3,QCSC-v1|7.2,Rule-ID|SV-280933r1197217_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-001040,Vuln-ID|V-280933"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/dnf/dnf.conf"
      regex       : "^[\\s]*localpkg_gpgcheck[\\s]*="
      expect      : "^[\\s]*localpkg_gpgcheck[\\s]*=[\\s]*1[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type            : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description     : "1.13 RHEL-10-001050"
      info            : "RHEL 10 must have GNU Privacy Guard (GPG) signature verification enabled for all software repositories.

GROUP ID: V-280934RULE ID: SV-280934r1165157

Changes to any software components can have significant effects on the overall security of the operating system. This requirement ensures the software has not been tampered with and has been provided by a trusted vendor.

All software packages must be signed with a cryptographic key recognized and approved by the organization.

Verifying the authenticity of software prior to installation validates the integrity of the software package received from a vendor."
      solution        : "Configure RHEL 10 software repositories defined in \"/etc/yum.repos.d/\" to have \"gpgcheck\" enabled with the following command:

$ sudo sed -i 's/gpgcheck\\s*=.*/gpgcheck=1/g' /etc/yum.repos.d/*"
      reference       : "800-171|3.4.5,800-171r3|03.04.05,800-53|CM-5(3),800-53r5|CM-14,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-003992,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.2,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.4,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.19,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.31,ISO-27001-2022|A.8.32,ITSG-33|CM-5(3),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T3.2.3,NESA|T5.1.1,NESA|T5.6.1,NESA|T7.5.1,NESA|T7.5.3,NESA|T7.6.3,QCSC-v1|7.2,Rule-ID|SV-280934r1165157_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-001050,Vuln-ID|V-280934"
      see_also        : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file            : "/etc/yum.repos.d/*.repo"
      regex           : "^[\\s]*gpgcheck[\\s]*="
      expect          : "^[\\s]*gpgcheck[\\s]*=[\\s]*1[\\s]*$"
      file_required   : NO
      min_occurrences : "1"
      string_required : NO
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : RPM_CHECK
      description : "1.16 RHEL-10-200020"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must not have the \"telnet-server\" package installed.

GROUP ID: V-280944
RULE ID: SV-280944r1184749

It is detrimental for operating systems to provide, or install by default, functionality exceeding requirements or mission objectives. These unnecessary capabilities are often overlooked and therefore, may remain unsecure. They increase the risk to the platform by providing additional attack vectors.

The telnet service provides an unencrypted remote access service, which does not provide for the confidentiality and integrity of user passwords or the remote session. If a privileged user were to log in using this service, the privileged user password could be compromised.

Removing the \"telnet-server\" package decreases the risk of accidental (or intentional) activation of the telnet service."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to not have the \"telnet-server\" package installed with the following command:

$ sudo dnf -y remove telnet-server"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.6,800-171|3.4.7,800-171r3|03.04.06,800-171r3|03.04.06a.,800-53|CM-7,800-53|CM-7a.,800-53r5|CM-7a.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000381,CN-L3|7.1.3.5(c),CN-L3|8.1.4.4(a),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|CM-7,ITSG-33|CM-7a.,LEVEL|1A,NIAv2|SS15a,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,Rule-ID|SV-280944r1184749_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-200020,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-280944"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      rpm         : "telnet-server-0.0.0-0"
      operator    : "lte"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.2 RHEL-10-000510"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must implement cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure or modification of all information on local disk partitions that requires at-rest protection.

GROUP ID: V-280935RULE ID: SV-280935r1184775

RHEL 10 systems handling data that requires \"data-at-rest\" protections must employ cryptographic mechanisms to prevent unauthorized disclosure and modification of the information at rest.

Selection of a cryptographic mechanism is based on the need to protect the integrity of organizational information. The strength of the mechanism is commensurate with the security category and/or classification of the information. Organizations have the flexibility to either encrypt all information on storage devices (i.e., full disk encryption) or encrypt specific data structures (e.g., files, records, or fields).

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000405-GPOS-00184, SRG-OS-000185-GPOS-00079, SRG-OS-000404-GPOS-00183

NOTE: Nessus has provided the target output to assist in reviewing the benchmark to ensure target compliance."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to prevent unauthorized modification of all information at rest by using disk encryption.

Encrypting a partition in an already installed system is more difficult, because existing partitions will have to be resized and changed.

To encrypt an entire partition, dedicate a partition for encryption in the partition layout."
      reference   : "800-171|3.13.16,800-171r3|03.13.08,800-53|SC-28,800-53|SC-28(1),800-53r5|SC-28,800-53r5|SC-28(1),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-001199,CCI|CCI-002475,CCI|CCI-002476,CN-L3|8.1.4.7(b),CN-L3|8.1.4.8(b),CSF|PR.DS-1,CSF2.0|PR.DS-01,GDPR|32.1.a,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(iv),HIPAA|164.312(e)(2)(ii),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.10,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.33,ITSG-33|SC-28,ITSG-33|SC-28a.,ITSG-33|SC-28(1),LEVEL|1M,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|3.4,PCI-DSSv4.0|3.3.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|3.5.1,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|6.2,Rule-ID|SV-280935r1184775_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-000510,TBA-FIISB|28.1,Vuln-ID|V-280935"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      cmd         : "/sbin/blkid"
      expect      : "^Manual Review Required$"
      severity    : MEDIUM
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : RPM_CHECK
      description : "1.21 RHEL-10-200070"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must not have the \"tftp\" package installed.

GROUP ID: V-280949
RULE ID: SV-280949r1195338

It is detrimental for operating systems to provide, or install by default, functionality exceeding requirements or mission objectives. These unnecessary capabilities are often overlooked and therefore, may remain unsecure. They increase the risk to the platform by providing additional attack vectors.

If Trivial File Transfer Protocol (TFTP) is required for operational support (such as transmission of router configurations), its use must be documented with the information system security manager, restricted to only authorized personnel, and have access control rules established."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to not have the \"tftp\" package installed with the following command:

$ sudo dnf -y remove tftp"
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000197,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-280949r1195338_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-200070,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-280949"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      rpm         : "tftp-0.0.0-0"
      operator    : "lte"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : RPM_CHECK
      description : "1.23 RHEL-10-200090"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must not have a File Transfer Protocol (FTP) server package installed.

GROUP ID: V-280951
RULE ID: SV-280951r1184744

The FTP service provides an unencrypted remote access that does not provide for the confidentiality and integrity of user passwords or the remote session. If a privileged user were to log in using this service, the privileged user password could be compromised. Secure Shell (SSH) or other encrypted file transfer methods must be used in place of this service.

Removing the \"vsftpd\" package decreases the risk of accidental activation.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000074-GPOS-00042, SRG-OS-000095-GPOS-00049"
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to not have the FTP package installed with the following command (using \"vsftpd\" as an example):

$ sudo dnf -y remove vsftpd"
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.6,800-171|3.4.7,800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.04.06,800-171r3|03.04.06a.,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|CM-7,800-53|CM-7a.,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|CM-7a.,800-53r5|IA-5(1),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000197,CCI|CCI-000381,CN-L3|7.1.3.5(c),CN-L3|8.1.4.4(a),CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF|PR.PT-3,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|CM-7,ITSG-33|CM-7a.,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.2.3,NIAv2|SS15a,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.1,PCI-DSSv3.2.1|2.2.2,PCI-DSSv4.0|2.2.3,QCSC-v1|3.2,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-280951r1184744_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-200090,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-280951"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      rpm         : "vsftpd-0.0.0-0"
      operator    : "lte"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK_NOT
      description : "1.280 RHEL-10-600560"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must require users to provide a password for privilege escalation.

GROUP ID: V-281211
RULE ID: SV-281211r1166585

Without reauthentication, users may access resources or perform tasks for which they do not have authorization.

When operating systems provide the capability to escalate a functional capability, it is critical that the user reauthenticate.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000373-GPOS-00156, SRG-OS-000373-GPOS-00157, SRG-OS-000373-GPOS-00158"
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to not allow users to execute privileged actions without authenticating with a password.

Remove any occurrence of \"NOPASSWD\" found in the \"/etc/sudoers\" file or files in the \"/etc/sudoers.d\" directory:

$ sudo find /etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d -type f -exec sed -i '/NOPASSWD/ s/^/# /g' {} \\;"
      reference   : "800-171r3|03.05.01b.,800-53|IA-11,800-53r5|IA-11,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-002038,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(d),LEVEL|1A,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-281211r1166585_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-600560,Vuln-ID|V-281211"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/sudoers /etc/sudoers.d/*"
      regex       : "(?i)^[^#\\n\\r]*nopasswd\\b"
      expect      : "(?i)^[^#\\n\\r]*nopasswd\\b"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.285 RHEL-10-600640"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must enable the Pluggable Authentication Module (PAM) interface for SSHD.

GROUP ID: V-281216RULE ID: SV-281216r1166600

When \"UsePAM\" is set to \"yes\", PAM runs through account and session types properly. This is important when restricted access to services based on IP, time, or other factors of the account is needed. Additionally, this ensures users can inherit certain environment variables on login or disallow access to the server."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 SSHD to use the \"UsePAM\" interface by adding or modifying the following line in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config\" or in a file in \"/etc/ssh/sshd_config.d\".

UsePAM yes

Restart the SSH daemon with the following command for the settings to take effect:

$ sudo systemctl restart sshd.service"
      reference   : "800-171|3.7.5,800-171r3|03.07.05b.,800-53|MA-4c.,800-53r5|MA-4c.,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000877,CSF|PR.MA-2,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|MA-4c.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|T2.3.4,NESA|T5.4.4,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,Rule-ID|SV-281216r1166600_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-600640,TBA-FIISB|45.2.3,Vuln-ID|V-281216"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      cmd         : "/sbin/sshd -T | /bin/grep -i UsePAM"
      expect      : "(?i)[\\s]*UsePAM[\\s]+\"?yes\"?[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.290 RHEL-10-600730"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must employ FIPS 140-3-approved cryptographic hashing algorithms for all stored passwords.

GROUP ID: V-281221RULE ID: SV-281221r1166615

The system must use a strong hashing algorithm to store the password.

Passwords must be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000073-GPOS-00041, SRG-OS-000120-GPOS-00061"
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to employ FIPS 140-3-approved cryptographic hashing algorithms for all stored passwords.

Lock all interactive user accounts not using SHA-512 hashing until the passwords can be regenerated with SHA-512."
      reference   : "800-53|IA-7,800-53r5|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-7,CAT|I,CCI|CCI-000803,CCI|CCI-004062,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(d),ITSG-33|IA-7,ITSG-33|IA-7a.,LEVEL|1A,NESA|M5.2.1,NESA|M5.2.6,NESA|M5.3.1,NESA|T7.4.1,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-281221r1166615_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-600730,Vuln-ID|V-281221"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      cmd         : "/bin/cut -d: -f1,2 /etc/shadow | /bin/grep -Pv ':[*!\\$]' | /bin/grep -Pv '^\\$6\\$' | /bin/awk -F: '{print $1} END {if (NR == 0) print \"All interactive user passwords are encrypted\"; else print \"There are interactive user passwords that are not encrypted\"}'"
      expect      : "^All interactive user passwords are encrypted$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
      description : "1.291 RHEL-10-600740"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must be configured to use the shadow file to store only encrypted representations of passwords.

GROUP ID: V-281222RULE ID: SV-281222r1184626

Passwords must be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised. Passwords that are encrypted with a weak algorithm are no more protected than if they are kept in plain text.

This setting ensures user and group account administration utilities are configured to store only encrypted representations of passwords. Additionally, the \"crypt_style\" configuration option ensures the use of a strong hashing algorithm that makes password cracking attacks more difficult."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to store only SHA512 encrypted representations of passwords.

Add or update the following line in the \"/etc/login.defs\" file:

ENCRYPT_METHOD SHA512"
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-004062,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-281222r1184626_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-600740,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-281222"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      file        : "/etc/login.defs"
      regex       : "(?i)^[\\s]*ENCRYPT_METHOD"
      expect      : "(?i)^[\\s]*ENCRYPT_METHOD[\\s]+@PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION@[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "1.292 RHEL-10-600750"
      info        : "RHEL 10 must be configured so that user and group account administration utilities are configured to store only encrypted representations of passwords.

GROUP ID: V-281223RULE ID: SV-281223r1166621

Passwords must be protected at all times, and encryption is the standard method for protecting passwords. If passwords are not encrypted, they can be plainly read (i.e., clear text) and easily compromised. Passwords that are encrypted with a weak algorithm are no more protected than if they are kept in plain text.

This setting ensures user and group account administration utilities are configured to store only encrypted representations of passwords. Additionally, the \"crypt_style\" configuration option ensures the use of a strong hashing algorithm that makes password cracking attacks more difficult."
      solution    : "Configure RHEL 10 to use the SHA-512 algorithm for password hashing.

Add or change the following line in the \"[default]\" section of the \"/etc/libuser.conf\" file:

crypt_style = sha512"
      reference   : "800-171|3.5.2,800-171r3|03.05.07,800-53|IA-5(1),800-53r5|IA-5(1),CAT|I,CCI|CCI-004062,CSF|PR.AC-1,CSF2.0|PR.AA-01,CSF2.0|PR.AA-03,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),HIPAA|164.312(a)(2)(i),HIPAA|164.312(d),ISO-27001-2022|A.5.16,ISO-27001-2022|A.5.17,ITSG-33|IA-5(1),LEVEL|1A,NESA|T5.2.3,QCSC-v1|5.2.2,QCSC-v1|13.2,Rule-ID|SV-281223r1166621_rule,STIG-ID|RHEL-10-600750,SWIFT-CSCv1|4.1,Vuln-ID|V-281223"
      see_also    : "https://workbench.cisecurity.org/benchmarks/26403"
      cmd         : "/bin/awk 'BEGIN {found=0} /^\\[/{in_defaults=0}/^\\s*\\[defaults/{in_defaults=1}/^\\s*crypt_style/{if(in_defaults==1){found=1;print \"Found in defaults: \"$0}} END {if(found==0){print \"crypt_style not found in defaults or not set\"}}' /etc/libuser.conf"
      expect      : "(?i)^[\\s]*Found in defaults:[\\s]*crypt_style[\\s]*=[\\s]*@PASSWORD_ENCRYPTION@[\\s]*$"
    </custom_item>

